st arboricity, introduced by Zhang in 2016, generalizes the notion of equitable list coloring by requiring the subgraph induced by each color class to be acyclic (instead of edgeless) in addition to the usual upper bound on the size of each color class. Graph $G$ is equitably $k$-list arborable if an equitable, arborable list coloring of $G$ exists for every list assignment for $G$ that associates with each vertex in $G$ a list of $k$ available colors. Zhang conjectured that any graph $G$ is equitably $k$-list arborable for each $k$ satisfying $k \\geq \\lceil (1+\\Delta(G))/2 \\rceil$. We verify this conjecture for powers of cycles by applying a new lemma which is a general tool for extending partial equitable, arborable list colorings. We also propose a stronger version of Zhang's Conjecture for certain connected graphs: any connected graph $G$ is equitably $k$-list arborable for each $k$ satisfying $k \\geq \\lceil \\Delta(G)/2 \\rceil$ provided $G$ is neither a cycle nor a complete graph of odd order. We verify this stronger version of Zhang's Conjecture for powers of paths, 2-degenerate graphs, and certain other graphs. We also show that if $G$ is equitably $k$-list arborable it does not necessarily follow that $G$ is equitably $(k+1)$-list arborable which addresses a question of Drgas-Burchardt, Furmanczyk, and Sidorowicz (2018).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nList colourings of planar graphs (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/295512654_List_colourings_of_planar_graphs)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 20.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; A graph G = G (V, E) is called L-list colourable if there is a vertex colouring of G in which the colour assigned to a vertex v is chosen from a list L (v) associated with this vertex. ... The list-coloring counterpart of the conjecture is also false: every counterexample to 4-choosability of an unsigned planar graph (e.g. Voigt [45]) gives, via the all-positive signature, a signed planar graph that is not signed 4-choosable. ... From the strengthened extension statement (\\cref{thm:main}) we deduce the main result (\\cref{thm:JKS}: \\chs\\Gs\\le 5 for every planar signed graph), the Máčajová--Raspaud--Škoviera signed Five-Color Theorem in the symmetric palette \\Ns{2}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}, the Switching Invariance Lemma, 3-choosability of outerplanar signed graphs, 1-defective signed 4-choosability of planar signed graphs, a sandwich inequality relating \\chs to the unsigned and positive'' choice numbers, and a polynomial-time list-coloring algorithm.\n\n## No full-text available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the author.\n\nRequest full-text PDF\n\n## Citations (180)\n\n## References (5)\n\n... We can now state the principal results. Voigt's planar non-4-choosable graph G 0 [45], viewed as (G 0 , +1), satisfies χ s ℓ (G 0 , +1) ≥ 5, so the signed list chromatic number can reach 5 already on planar signed graphs (see also Mirzakhani [29]). The complementary upper bound was established by Jin, Kang, and Steffen. ...\n\n... Example 6.1 (Voigt's planar graph as a signed graph). Voigt [45] constructed a planar graph G 0 on 238 vertices that is not 4-choosable, with explicit list assignment L of size 4 at every vertex. Endow G 0 with the all-positive signature σ 0 ≡ +1. ...\n\n... The list-coloring counterpart of the conjecture is also false: every counterexample to 4-choosability of an unsigned planar graph (e.g. Voigt [45]) gives, via the all-positive signature, a signed planar graph that is not signed 4-choosable. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n$4$-Colouring of Generalized Signed Planar Graphs (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/view/v27i3p31)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.2 years ago; Crawled: 4 days ago; # $4$-Colouring of Generalized Signed Planar Graphs ... This paper studies the problem as to which subsets $S$ of $S_4$, every  planar graph is $S$-$4$-colourable.\n\n# $4$-Colouring of Generalized Signed Planar Graphs\n\n  * Yiting Jiang\n  * Xuding Zhu\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.37236/9338\n\n### Abstract\n\nAssume $G$ is a graph and $S$ is a set of permutations of positive integers. An $S$-signature of $G$ is a pair $(D, \\sigma)$, where $D$ is an orientation of $G$ and $\\sigma: E(D) \\to S$ is a mapping which assigns to each arc $e=(u,v)$ a permutation $\\sigma(e)$ in $S$. We say $G$ is $S$-$k$-colourable if for any $S$-signature $(D, \\sigma)$ of $G$, there is a mapping $f: V(G) \\to [k]$ such that for each arc $e=(u,v)$ of $G$, $\\sigma(e)(f(u)) \\ne f(v)$. The concept of $S$-$k$-colourable is a common generalization of many colouring concepts. This paper studies the problem as to which subsets $S$ of $S_4$, every  planar graph is $S$-$4$-colourable. We call such a subset $S$ of $S_4$ a good subset. The Four Colour Theorem is equivalent to saying that $S=\\{id\\}$ is good. It was proved by Jin, Wong and Zhu (arXiv:1811.08584) that a subset $S$ containing $id$ is good if and only if $S=\\{id\\}$. In this paper, we prove that, up to conjugation, every good subset of $S_4$ not containing $id$ is a subset of $\\{(12),(34),(12)(34)\\}$.\n\nPublished\n\n2020-08-21\n\nHow to Cite\n\nJiang, Y., & Zhu, X. (2020). $4$-Colouring of Generalized Signed Planar Graphs. The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics, 27(3), #P3.31. https://doi.org/10.37236/9338\n\nIssue\n\nVolume 27, Issue 3 (2020)\n\nArticle Number\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSigned planar graphs with given circular chromatic numbers (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0012365X22002266)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Disproving a conjecture of Máčajová, Raspaud and Škoviera, Kardoš and Narboni constructed a non-4-colourable simple signed planar graph, or equivalently, a simple signed planar graph with circular chromatic number greater than 4. ... A signed graph is a graph G together with a signature σ which assigns to each edge e a sign . ... It is natural to ask if the Four Colour Theorem can be extended to simple signed planar graphs. ... In this paper, we further strengthen this result and prove the following theorem.\n\n## Discrete Mathematics\n\nDate: November 2022\n\nArticle: 113020\n\nVolume: Volume 345, Issue 11\n\n# Signed planar graphs with given circular chromatic numbers\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Yangyan Gu, Xuding Zhu ^{1}\n\nShow more\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.disc.2022.113020\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nDisproving a conjecture of Máčajová, Raspaud and Škoviera, Kardoš and Narboni constructed a non-4-colourable simple signed planar graph, or equivalently, a simple signed planar graph with circular chromatic number greater than 4. Naserasr, Wang, and Zhu improved this result by constructing a simple signed planar graph with circular chromatic number 14/3. This paper further strengthens this result by constructing, for each rational , a simple signed planar graph with circular chromatic number . Together with some earlier results of Moser and Zhu, this implies that every rational is the circular chromatic number of a simple signed planar graph.\n\n## Keywords\n\nSigned planar graph\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nColouring of S -labelled planar graphs (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/343796839_Colouring_of_S_-labelled_planar_graphs)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; The later result combined with the above result of Kemnitz and Voigt disproves a conjecture of Kang and Steffen that every planar graph is signed Z4-colourable. We shall show that a graph constructed by Wegner in 1973 is also a counterexample to Kang and Steffen's conjecture, and present a new construction of a non-{1,3}-choosable planar graphs.\nA very recent result of Kemnitz and Voigt implies that for any partition λ of 4 other than {1,1,1,1}, there is a planar graph which is not λ-choosable. We observe that, in contrast to the fact that there are non-4-choosable 3-chromatic planar graphs, every 3-chromatic planar graph is {1,3}-choosable, and that if G is a planar graph whose dual G⁎ has a connected spanning Eulerian subgraph, then G is {2,2}-choosable. We prove that if n is a positive even integer, λ is a partition of n−1 in which each part is at most 3, then Kn is edge λ-choosable. Finally we study relations between λ-choosability of graphs and colouring of signed graphs and generalized signed graphs. A conjecture of Máčajová, Raspaud and Škoviera that every planar graph is signed 4-colcourable is recently disproved by Kardoš and Narboni. We prove that every signed 4-colourable graph is weakly 4-choosable, and every signed Z4-colourable graph is {1,1,2}-choosable. The later result combined with the above result of Kemnitz and Voigt disproves a conjecture of Kang and Steffen that every planar graph is signed Z4-colourable. We shall show that a graph constructed by Wegner in 1973 is also a counterexample to Kang and Steffen's conjecture, and present a new construction of a non-{1,3}-choosable planar graphs.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Alon-Tarsi number of a planar graph minus a matching (https://doi.org/10.1016%2Fj.jctb.2020.02.005)\nciteturn1search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; In the proof of Theorem 6, in Case 2, instead of adding the edge 𝑢𝑖⁢𝑣𝑛 to the matching M, we may increase the power of 𝑢𝑖 by 1. ... A signed graph is a pair (𝐺,𝜎), where G is a graph and σ is a signature of G which assigns to each edge e of G a sign 𝜎𝑒 ∈{1,−1}. ... If (𝐺,𝜎) is a signed planar graph, then G has a matching M such that 𝐴⁢𝑇⁡(𝐺− 𝑀,𝜎) ≤4. ... Every signed planar graph (𝐺,𝜎) has a subset X of vertices with |𝑋|< |𝑉⁡(𝐺)|/2 such that 